[2026-03-15T08:58:11.844Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K93sWnNHeWHphNLjzbxA7P","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# preflight\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→\n     5→Standard pre-test and pre-deploy checklist to run before executing `npm run test` or\n     6→`npm run deploy`. This sequence was independently converged on across many debugging sessions\n     7→and should be followed every time.\n     8→\n     9→## Pre-Test Preflight\n    10→\n    11→Run these steps from the app directory **once** before each test session (not before every\n    12→individual test file). Steps 2–3 (env/browser verification) and step 5 (deps) do not change\n    13→between runs within the same session — re-checking them before every test file is redundant\n    14→and wastes ~200+ commands per session. Only step 1 (kill stale processes) and step 4 (clear\n    15→recordings) need to run before each `npm run test` invocation.\n    16→\n    17→### 1. Kill stale servers\n    18→\n    19→```bash\n    20→ps aux | grep -E \"netlify|vite\" | grep -v grep | awk '{print $2}' | xargs -r kill 2>/dev/null; echo \"servers cleared\"\n    21→```\n    22→\n    23→Stale `netlify dev` and `vite` processes from previous runs cause port conflicts and\n    24→serve outdated code. Always kill them before starting tests.\n    25→\n    26→**Important**: Do NOT use `pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true` as the primary\n    27→approach — `pkill` exits 0 even when no process is found, so `|| true` makes it look\n    28→successful even when servers are still running. The `ps aux | xargs kill` pipeline is more\n    29→reliable. See `skills/scripts/killStaleProcesses.md` for details and fallback approaches.\n    30→\n    31→**Port recovery**: If the port is still occupied after killing processes (zombie processes\n    32→invisible to `ps`), use `fuser` to force-free it:\n    33→\n    34→```bash\n    35→fuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n    36→```\n    37→\n    38→This should be a standard fallback when `netlify dev` fails to bind a port.\n    39→\n    40→**Run each preflight step as a separate command**, not as a combined one-liner. The pattern\n    41→`cd X && pkill ... && grep ... && ls ...` fails if any sub-command fails (especially pkill\n    42→when no process exists), wasting effort decomposing and re-running individual commands.\n    43→\n    44→**Do NOT re-run individual checks after a combined check passes.** If you run all preflight\n    45→steps and they succeed, do not then re-run each step individually as a \"double check\". This\n    46→pattern wastes 3-5 commands per test run and provides no additional value. Trust the output\n    47→of each step — if it passed, move on.\n    48→\n    49→### 1b. Check disk space\n    50→\n    51→```bash\n    52→df -h / | awk 'NR==2 {print $4}'\n    53→```\n    54→\n    55→If free space is below 1GB, run the disk cleanup procedure in `skills/scripts/disk-cleanup.md`\n    56→before proceeding. Disk exhaustion (0 bytes free) causes cascading failures: dev server crashes,\n    57→Replay browser deletion, and npm install failures.\n    58→\n    59→### 2. Verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` in `.env`\n    60→\n    61→```bash\n    62→grep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env\n    63→```\n    64→\n    65→The test script requires `NEON_PROJECT_ID` for creating ephemeral Neon branches. If missing,\n    66→check `deployment.txt` for the project ID and populate `.env`. See `skills/scripts/env-setup.md`.\n    67→\n    68→### 3. Verify Replay browser\n    69→\n    70→```bash\n    71→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n    72→```\n    73→\n    74→If the Replay browser is not installed, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution.\n    75→Without it, test failures produce no recordings and debugging is impossible.\n    76→\n    77→### 4. Clear stale Replay recordings\n    78→\n    79→```bash\n    80→npx replayio remove --all\n    81→```\n    82→\n    83→Old recordings from prior runs cause confusion when parsing results. Always clear them\n    84→before starting a new test run.\n    85→\n    86→### 5. Verify dependencies\n    87→\n    88→```bash\n    89→ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n    90→```\n    91→\n    92→Use `--legacy-peer-deps` to avoid peer dependency conflicts with `netlify-cli` and ESLint\n    93→packages. This is the standard approach for `npm install` in this ecosystem.\n    94→\n    95→## Pre-Deploy Preflight\n    96→\n    97→### 1. Populate `.env` from `deployment.txt`\n    98→\n    99→If `.env` is missing or incomplete, read `deployment.txt` for `site_id`, `neon_project_id`,\n   100→and `database_url`. See `skills/scripts/deploy.md` § \"Populating `.env` for Redeployments\".\n   101→\n   102→### 2. Verify container-level env vars\n   103→\n   104→```bash\n   105→echo $NEON_API_KEY | head -c 5\n   106→echo $NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN | head -c 5\n   107→```\n   108→\n   109→See `skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for the full list of required variables.\n   110→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c4da05b-f48b-41c2-b979-f34be8c3cef2","uuid":"ecf8f8dd-6b50-444a-bcb0-a6a7a2126741","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/scripts/preflight.md","content":"# preflight\n\n## Purpose\n\nStandard pre-test and pre-deploy checklist to run before executing `npm run test` or\n`npm run deploy`. This sequence was independently converged on across many debugging sessions\nand should be followed every time.\n\n## Pre-Test Preflight\n\nRun these steps from the app directory **once** before each test session (not before every\nindividual test file). Steps 2–3 (env/browser verification) and step 5 (deps) do not change\nbetween runs within the same session — re-checking them before every test file is redundant\nand wastes ~200+ commands per session. Only step 1 (kill stale processes) and step 4 (clear\nrecordings) need to run before each `npm run test` invocation.\n\n### 1. Kill stale servers\n\n```bash\nps aux | grep -E \"netlify|vite\" | grep -v grep | awk '{print $2}' | xargs -r kill 2>/dev/null; echo \"servers cleared\"\n```\n\nStale `netlify dev` and `vite` processes from previous runs cause port conflicts and\nserve outdated code. Always kill them before starting tests.\n\n**Important**: Do NOT use `pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true` as the primary\napproach — `pkill` exits 0 even when no process is found, so `|| true` makes it look\nsuccessful even when servers are still running. The `ps aux | xargs kill` pipeline is more\nreliable. See `skills/scripts/killStaleProcesses.md` for details and fallback approaches.\n\n**Port recovery**: If the port is still occupied after killing processes (zombie processes\ninvisible to `ps`), use `fuser` to force-free it:\n\n```bash\nfuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n```\n\nThis should be a standard fallback when `netlify dev` fails to bind a port.\n\n**Run each preflight step as a separate command**, not as a combined one-liner. The pattern\n`cd X && pkill ... && grep ... && ls ...` fails if any sub-command fails (especially pkill\nwhen no process exists), wasting effort decomposing and re-running individual commands.\n\n**Do NOT re-run individual checks after a combined check passes.** If you run all preflight\nsteps and they succeed, do not then re-run each step individually as a \"double check\". This\npattern wastes 3-5 commands per test run and provides no additional value. Trust the output\nof each step — if it passed, move on.\n\n### 1b. Check disk space\n\n```bash\ndf -h / | awk 'NR==2 {print $4}'\n```\n\nIf free space is below 1GB, run the disk cleanup procedure in `skills/scripts/disk-cleanup.md`\nbefore proceeding. Disk exhaustion (0 bytes free) causes cascading failures: dev server crashes,\nReplay browser deletion, and npm install failures.\n\n### 2. Verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` in `.env`\n\n```bash\ngrep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env\n```\n\nThe test script requires `NEON_PROJECT_ID` for creating ephemeral Neon branches. If missing,\ncheck `deployment.txt` for the project ID and populate `.env`. See `skills/scripts/env-setup.md`.\n\n### 3. Verify Replay browser\n\n```bash\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n```\n\nIf the Replay browser is not installed, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution.\nWithout it, test failures produce no recordings and debugging is impossible.\n\n### 4. Clear stale Replay recordings\n\n```bash\nnpx replayio remove --all\n```\n\nOld recordings from prior runs cause confusion when parsing results. Always clear them\nbefore starting a new test run.\n\n### 5. Verify dependencies\n\n```bash\nls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n```\n\nUse `--legacy-peer-deps` to avoid peer dependency conflicts with `netlify-cli` and ESLint\npackages. This is the standard approach for `npm install` in this ecosystem.\n\n## Pre-Deploy Preflight\n\n### 1. Populate `.env` from `deployment.txt`\n\nIf `.env` is missing or incomplete, read `deployment.txt` for `site_id`, `neon_project_id`,\nand `database_url`. See `skills/scripts/deploy.md` § \"Populating `.env` for Redeployments\".\n\n### 2. Verify container-level env vars\n\n```bash\necho $NEON_API_KEY | head -c 5\necho $NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN | head -c 5\n```\n\nSee `skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for the full list of required variables.\n","numLines":110,"startLine":1,"totalLines":110}}}
